pad: recheck FLUSHING after the push probes before handing an item to the peer

Probes run without the pad lock.  A FLUSH_START arriving meanwhile flushes
only the peer the pad had at that moment; when a probe relinks the pad
(decodebin3 builds the decoder output from its CAPS probe on the multiqueue
source pad) the streaming thread then carried the event into the new,
never-flushed chain, blocked in playsink's pad block, and the flushing
thread waited forever in gst_pad_pause_task() for the stream lock that
thread holds: the MSE seek hang in the web process.

--- a/gst/gstpad.c
+++ b/gst/gstpad.c
@@ -4759,6 +4759,11 @@
   if (G_UNLIKELY ((ret = check_sticky (pad, NULL))) != GST_FLOW_OK)
     goto events_error;
 
+  /* A flush may have started while the probes ran without the pad lock and
+   * missed a peer that a probe linked meanwhile; do not hand data to it. */
+  if (G_UNLIKELY (GST_PAD_IS_FLUSHING (pad)))
+    goto flushing;
+
   if (G_UNLIKELY ((peer = GST_PAD_PEER (pad)) == NULL))
     goto not_linked;
 
@@ -5592,6 +5597,20 @@
         pad->offset - old_pad_offset);
   }
 
+  /* The probes ran without the pad lock.  A FLUSH_START that arrived
+   * meanwhile set FLUSHING here and went on to whatever peer the pad had
+   * at that moment; if a probe relinked the pad (decodebin3 does that from
+   * its CAPS probe), the new peer never saw the flush.  Forwarding the
+   * event anyway would carry this streaming thread into an unflushed
+   * downstream chain, where a blocking probe can hold it forever while the
+   * flushing thread waits for the stream lock that this thread owns.  The
+   * flushing check above only covers the moment before the probes, so
+   * repeat it now. */
+  if (event_type != GST_EVENT_FLUSH_START
+      && event_type != GST_EVENT_FLUSH_STOP
+      && G_UNLIKELY (GST_PAD_IS_FLUSHING (pad)))
+    goto flushed;
+
   /* now check the peer pad */
   peerpad = GST_PAD_PEER (pad);
   if (peerpad == NULL)
